PG.notes("l149", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces jQuery as the most widely used JavaScript library and shows why it exists: it collapses verbose DOM code into short expressions such as $(&quot;h1&quot;). It also notes that a library is simply someone else's code you pull into your project, like Bootstrap.</p>\n<h3>What a library is</h3>\n<ul><li>A library is a bundle of code written by someone else that you include in your project to save effort. Bootstrap, used earlier for CSS/JS components, is the same idea.</li><li>jQuery was created by John Resig because plain DOM scripting felt long-winded for simple jobs. It became the most downloaded JavaScript library.</li><li>Later sections of the course meet other libraries too, so this is the first example of a pattern: add a library, then call its functions.</li></ul>\n<h3>The problem jQuery solves</h3>\n<ul><li>The comparison example is a drum-kit style snippet: query all buttons, loop over them with a for loop, attach a click listener to each, and inside the callback query the h1 and set its style.color to red.</li><li>In plain JavaScript each step needs its own call (querySelectorAll, loop, addEventListener, querySelector, .style.color), so a trivial behaviour takes many lines.</li><li>jQuery condenses the selecting, looping over matches and event wiring into a few short calls, so code is quicker to write, easier to read and easier to debug.</li></ul>\n<h3>The jQuery function and the $ shorthand</h3>\n<ul><li>jQuery(&quot;h1&quot;) looks up elements by a CSS selector, much as document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;) does.</li><li>$ is a shorthand alias for jQuery, so $(&quot;h1&quot;) and jQuery(&quot;h1&quot;) are identical.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Unlike querySelector, the jQuery selection can hold many elements and methods called on it apply to all of them, which is why the explicit for loop disappears.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Plain JavaScript version: listener on every button, turns the h1 red</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var buttons = document.querySelectorAll(&quot;button&quot;);\nfor (var i = 0; i &lt; buttons.length; i++) {\n  buttons[i].addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function () {\n    document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).style.color = &quot;red&quot;;\n  });\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Selecting an element: vanilla vs jQuery</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;);   // vanilla\njQuery(&quot;h1&quot;);                   // full name\n$(&quot;h1&quot;);                        // shorthand, same thing</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The same behaviour with jQuery</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">$(&quot;button&quot;).click(function () {\n  $(&quot;h1&quot;).css(&quot;color&quot;, &quot;red&quot;);\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>jQuery code only works once the library has been loaded into the page; otherwise $ is undefined (covered in the next lecture).</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l150", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows how to add jQuery to a page, either by downloading it or via a CDN such as Google Hosted Libraries, builds a small starter project, and demonstrates why the position of the jQuery script tag matters. Also covers $(document).ready() as a guard when scripts sit in the head.</p>\n<h3>Getting jQuery: download or CDN</h3>\n<ul><li>jquery.com offers the library as a downloadable file or as a CDN link, in the same way Bootstrap does.</li><li>The most common choice is Google's CDN (Google Hosted Libraries). Copy its script tag for the jQuery version you want.</li><li>A popular CDN has a caching benefit: if the visitor already fetched that same URL while on another site, the browser reuses the cached copy and your page loads faster.</li><li>Use a current 3.x release; the lecture used 3.3.1 and suggests picking whatever is latest. The reference repo uses 3.7.1.</li></ul>\n<h3>Project setup used in the demo</h3>\n<ul><li>Create a project folder named jQuery inside the Web Development Projects folder with index.html, styles.css and index.js.</li><li>In index.html add the HTML boilerplate and a title of jQuery.</li><li>Link the stylesheet with a link element (rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot;, href=&quot;styles.css&quot;) in the head, just below the title.</li><li>Put the JavaScript script tag (src=&quot;index.js&quot;) at the end of the body, right before the closing body tag.</li><li>Smoke-test both links: set the body background to red in the CSS and call an alert in index.js. When both show up, delete the test code.</li><li>Add an h1 saying Hello and five buttons labelled Click Me for later lessons.</li></ul>\n<h3>First jQuery line and the missing-library error</h3>\n<ul><li>$(&quot;h1&quot;).css(&quot;color&quot;, &quot;red&quot;); selects the h1 and sets its colour property. The details of css() come in later lectures.</li><li>Without the library loaded, the console reports that $ is not defined, since $ only exists once jQuery has run.</li><li>To fix it, paste the CDN script tag into the page above the script tag for index.js. After a refresh the error disappears and the heading turns red.</li></ul>\n<h3>Script order matters</h3>\n<ul><li>Browsers process script tags top to bottom. If the jQuery tag comes after index.js, index.js runs first, meets an unknown $ and fails with the same not defined error.</li><li>Rule: the jQuery script tag must come before any of your own scripts that use it.</li><li>If both script tags sit in the head, the page can fail silently (no helpful error), because your code may run before the document body exists or before the library has finished loading.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The cause with head scripts is mostly that the h1 is not yet in the DOM when the selector runs, so nothing is matched.</li></ul>\n<h3>$(document).ready()</h3>\n<ul><li>Wrapping code in $(document).ready(function () { ... }) delays it until the document is ready, so it works even if scripts are in the head.</li><li>You will often see this pattern in other people's code.</li><li>The alternative, used for the rest of the course, is to place all scripts at the end of the body. That has the same effect, so the ready() wrapper is not needed in later lessons.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Modern shorthand: $(function () { ... }) does the same as $(document).ready(...).</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">index.html: stylesheet in head, jQuery before own script at end of body</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;meta charset=&quot;UTF-8&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;jQuery&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;styles.css&quot;&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;button&gt;Click Me&lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;button&gt;Click Me&lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;button&gt;Click Me&lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;button&gt;Click Me&lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;button&gt;Click Me&lt;/button&gt;\n\n  &lt;script src=&quot;https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/jquery/3.7.1/jquery.min.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n  &lt;script src=&quot;index.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">index.js: first jQuery code</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">$(&quot;h1&quot;).css(&quot;color&quot;, &quot;red&quot;);\n\n// jQuery(...) is the long form of $(...)\njQuery(&quot;h2&quot;).css(&quot;color&quot;, &quot;blue&quot;);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Guarding with ready() when scripts are in the head</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">$(document).ready(function () {\n  $(&quot;h1&quot;).css(&quot;color&quot;, &quot;red&quot;);\n});</pre>\n<h2>The task: Link CSS and JS into the new jQuery project</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Empty project folder with index.html, styles.css and index.js and boilerplate in index.html.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Link the stylesheet and script correctly, then get jQuery working through the CDN.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Pause and add a link element for styles.css in the head, and a script element for index.js before the closing body tag.</li><li>Prove both work with a red body background and an alert.</li><li>Add an h1 and five buttons.</li><li>Write $(&quot;h1&quot;).css(&quot;color&quot;, &quot;red&quot;); in index.js and observe the $ is not defined console error.</li><li>Add the Google CDN script tag above the index.js tag; refresh and confirm the heading is red.</li><li>Experiment: move the jQuery tag below index.js, then move both scripts into the head, and see each fail. Fix the head case with $(document).ready().</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Red h1 with no console errors when jQuery is loaded before index.js at the end of the body.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>jQuery script tag placed after your own script gives $ is not defined.</li><li>Both scripts in the head can fail without a clear error; use $(document).ready() or move scripts to the end of body.</li><li>Open the file from its real path or live preview when testing; refresh after each change.</li><li>The reference repo's index.html has a personal author footer (ignore it); its h2 example is extra to the lecture.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l151", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains what minified code is, why CDN-hosted libraries like jQuery and Bootstrap look like unreadable walls of text, and how a minifier tool shrinks JS or CSS by stripping whitespace and comments.</p>\n<h3>What minification is</h3>\n<ul><li>Opening a CDN URL for jquery.min.js (or the Bootstrap CSS) shows a dense blob. That is the minified build: the same code with spaces, line breaks, indentation and comments removed.</li><li>It is still valid JavaScript (for jQuery) or CSS (for Bootstrap). Reformatting it with line breaks and indentation reveals the ordinary code.</li><li>Browsers ignore whitespace and comments, so shipping them only wastes bytes.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why minify</h3>\n<ul><li>Smaller files download faster, which matters most on slow connections.</li><li>Demo: a minifier website (minifier.org) takes pasted JavaScript or CSS and outputs the compressed version. The sample drum-kit script went from about 1539 bytes to about 1113 bytes, saving roughly 426 bytes, even with just one added comment.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Real minifiers also shorten local variable names, which saves more; savings grow with file size.</li></ul>\n<h3>Library distributions</h3>\n<ul><li>Libraries ship two versions: a readable, uncompressed one for developers who want to study or customise the source, and a minified one meant for the browser.</li><li>If you do not need to edit the library, drop the minified file in (or link the CDN version).</li><li>If you customise the source, you can minify it yourself afterwards.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Minified files usually carry .min in the name (jquery.min.js); keep your own source readable and minify only for production.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Same logic, readable vs minified</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">// readable\nfunction add(a, b) {\n  // add two numbers\n  return a + b;\n}\n\n// minified\nfunction add(n,d){return n+d}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Do not hand-edit a minified file; edit the readable source and minify again.</li><li>Comments you rely on will vanish after minification, so keep an unminified original.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l152", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows that $() takes any CSS selector and replaces both querySelector and querySelectorAll, returning a jQuery object whether one or many elements match.</p>\n<h3>Selecting with $()</h3>\n<ul><li>$(&quot;h1&quot;) replaces document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;); the dollar sign stands for jQuery.</li><li>The argument is a standard CSS selector string: type selectors (&quot;h1&quot;), class selectors (&quot;.title&quot;), id selectors (&quot;#header&quot;), combinators such as descendant (&quot;#header h1&quot;) and compound selectors (&quot;h1.title&quot;). Selector syntax is unchanged from CSS.</li><li>CSS selectors were chosen because they are flexible and can be as specific as needed.</li></ul>\n<h3>One call for one or many</h3>\n<ul><li>In vanilla JS you must choose querySelector (first match) or querySelectorAll (all matches). jQuery has a single form, so $(&quot;button&quot;) grabs all buttons.</li><li>Methods called on the result apply to every matched element, so no explicit loop is needed.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The returned value is a jQuery object (array-like wrapper), not a raw DOM node; logging it in the console shows a list of matches. Use $(&quot;h1&quot;)[0] or .get(0) to reach the raw element.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Selecting in jQuery and logging the results (matches the reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">console.log($(&quot;h1&quot;));\nconsole.log($(&quot;button&quot;));   // all five buttons in one object</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Selector variety</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">$(&quot;h1.title&quot;);\n$(&quot;#header h1&quot;);\n$(&quot;.title&quot;);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>The result is a jQuery object, not a DOM element: DOM properties like .style or .innerText are not available directly on it; use jQuery methods instead.</li><li>Selecting is silent when nothing matches: you get an empty object rather than an error.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l153", "<p class=\"summary\">Covers the jQuery .css() getter/setter and the class methods addClass, removeClass and hasClass, arguing that toggling classes is cleaner than setting inline styles because it keeps behaviour and appearance separate.</p>\n<h3>.css() as setter and getter</h3>\n<ul><li>$(&quot;h1&quot;).css(&quot;color&quot;, &quot;green&quot;) sets a CSS property: first argument is the property name, second the value, both as strings.</li><li>With only one argument, $(&quot;h1&quot;).css(&quot;color&quot;) reads the current value instead. For colour, the result is an rgb(...) string (black appears as rgb(0, 0, 0)).</li><li>Reading &quot;font-size&quot; returns the computed size in pixels (32px for a default h1 in the demo).</li><li>Rule of thumb: one argument gets, two arguments set.</li></ul>\n<h3>Keep concerns separate: use classes</h3>\n<ul><li>Setting styles from JavaScript mixes appearance into behaviour code. Preferred split: HTML for content, CSS for appearance, JS for behaviour.</li><li>Define a class in the stylesheet (demo: .big-title with a large font-size, yellow colour and cursive font-family) and let jQuery attach it.</li><li>$(&quot;h1&quot;).addClass(&quot;big-title&quot;) adds the class; $(&quot;h1&quot;).removeClass(&quot;big-title&quot;) takes it away and the rule's styles disappear.</li><li>To add or remove several classes in one call, put them in a single string separated by spaces: addClass(&quot;big-title margin-50&quot;).</li><li>$(&quot;h1&quot;).hasClass(&quot;margin-50&quot;) returns true or false depending on whether the element carries that class.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> toggleClass(&quot;name&quot;) adds the class if absent and removes it if present; handy with event handlers.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">style.css</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.big-title {\n  font-size: 5rem;\n  color: yellow;\n  font-family: cursive;\n}\n\n.margin-50 {\n  margin: 50px;\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">script.js: css(), addClass, removeClass, hasClass</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">$(&quot;h1&quot;).css(&quot;color&quot;, &quot;green&quot;);        // set\nconsole.log($(&quot;h1&quot;).css(&quot;color&quot;));    // get -&gt; rgb(...)\nconsole.log($(&quot;h1&quot;).css(&quot;font-size&quot;)); // get -&gt; e.g. 32px\n\n$(&quot;h1&quot;).addClass(&quot;big-title margin-50&quot;);\n$(&quot;h1&quot;).removeClass(&quot;big-title&quot;);\nconsole.log($(&quot;h1&quot;).hasClass(&quot;margin-50&quot;)); // true</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Separate multiple class names with a space inside ONE string, not as separate arguments or with dots.</li><li>Do not put a dot before the class name in addClass/removeClass/hasClass (use &quot;big-title&quot;, not &quot;.big-title&quot;).</li><li>The reference repo calls removeClass before the class has been added; harmless but not what the lecture sequence shows.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l154", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows the two jQuery methods for changing element content: .text() for plain text and .html() for content that includes markup. Both act on every matched element.</p>\n<h3>.text()</h3>\n<ul><li>$(&quot;h1&quot;).text(&quot;Bye&quot;) replaces the content of the h1 with that string.</li><li>Because a jQuery selection covers all matches, $(&quot;button&quot;).text(&quot;Don't Click Me&quot;) updates all five buttons in one statement; no loop is needed.</li><li>Any HTML tags in the string are treated as literal text, not markup.</li></ul>\n<h3>.html()</h3>\n<ul><li>$(&quot;h1&quot;).html(&quot;&lt;em&gt;Bye&lt;/em&gt;&quot;) parses the string as HTML, so the em tag renders the word in italics.</li><li>It is the jQuery equivalent of setting innerHTML in vanilla JavaScript; .text() corresponds roughly to textContent / innerText.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Avoid passing untrusted user input to .html(), as it can inject scripts; use .text() for that.</li></ul>\n<h3>How jQuery relates to JavaScript</h3>\n<ul><li>Almost everything in jQuery is a method reached with dot notation, such as .css, .addClass, .text.</li><li>jQuery is itself written in JavaScript; its methods are wrappers that shorten common tasks, in the same spirit as writing your own functions to cut repetition.</li><li>Understanding vanilla JavaScript first makes jQuery understandable rather than magic.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Text and HTML (matches reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">$(&quot;h1&quot;).text(&quot;Bye&quot;);\n$(&quot;h1&quot;).html(&quot;&lt;em&gt;Bye&lt;/em&gt;&quot;);\n$(&quot;button&quot;).text(&quot;Don't Click Me&quot;);\n$(&quot;button&quot;).html(&quot;&lt;em&gt;Don't Click Me&lt;/em&gt;&quot;);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>.text() will show tags literally, e.g. an em tag appears as visible characters.</li><li>Calling .text(value) with an argument is a setter; with no argument it returns the current text.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l155", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces .attr() for reading and writing HTML attributes such as img src and a href, noting that the class attribute can be read the same way.</p>\n<h3>.attr() as getter and setter</h3>\n<ul><li>Attributes are the name/value pairs inside an opening tag: src on an img, href on an a, class on almost anything.</li><li>One argument reads: $(&quot;img&quot;).attr(&quot;src&quot;) returns the attribute value, e.g. the file name drum.png. Useful for diagnosing a broken image (the logged path shows what the browser tried to load).</li><li>Two arguments write: $(&quot;a&quot;).attr(&quot;href&quot;, &quot;https://www.yahoo.com&quot;) changes every selected anchor's destination. Clicking the link then goes to the new URL.</li><li>Same rule as .css(): with a second argument it sets; without one it gets.</li></ul>\n<h3>Class is an attribute too</h3>\n<ul><li>$(&quot;h1&quot;).attr(&quot;class&quot;) returns the full space-separated class string, e.g. big-title margin-50.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> For adding or removing individual classes prefer addClass/removeClass; attr(&quot;class&quot;, ...) would overwrite the whole list.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> When getting, jQuery returns the value of the first matched element; when setting, it applies to all matches.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Get and set href (matches reference repo, which uses Bing)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">console.log($(&quot;a&quot;).attr(&quot;href&quot;));          // current URL\n$(&quot;a&quot;).attr(&quot;href&quot;, &quot;https://www.bing.com&quot;); // change it\n\nconsole.log($(&quot;h1&quot;).attr(&quot;class&quot;));       // e.g. big-title margin-50</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>attr(&quot;class&quot;, value) replaces all existing classes; use addClass to append.</li><li>A src that points at a missing file fails to load; logging attr(&quot;src&quot;) is a quick way to see the path being requested.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l156", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows how jQuery attaches event listeners with shortcut methods (.click, .keypress) and the general .on() method, and how a single selection applies the handler to every matched element without a loop. Includes a small challenge: show the pressed key inside the h1.</p>\n<h3>Click listeners with .click()</h3>\n<ul><li>$(&quot;h1&quot;).click(function () { ... }) registers a click listener on the h1; the function is the callback that runs on each click.</li><li>Inside the callback another jQuery call changes something, for example $(&quot;h1&quot;).css(&quot;color&quot;, &quot;purple&quot;).</li><li>Comparison with vanilla JS: to listen on all five buttons you need querySelectorAll, an index loop, addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, ...) and querySelector inside the callback, setting .style.color. Watch for typos; the editor's syntax colouring can hint that a function name is unknown.</li><li>With jQuery, $(&quot;button&quot;).click(function () { ... }) is enough, because the selection already contains every button and jQuery attaches the listener to each. No for loop.</li></ul>\n<h3>Keyboard events with .keypress()</h3>\n<ul><li>Add an input element to the HTML, then $(&quot;input&quot;).keypress(function (event) { console.log(event.key); }) logs the key pressed while typing in the box. The callback receives the event object; event.key holds the character or name, such as k or Enter.</li><li>To listen across the whole page instead of one input, select the body, or pass the document object itself, with no quotes: $(document).keypress(...). Click into the page first so it receives focus.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> keypress is deprecated in browsers; keydown (also available as $(document).keydown(...), which the reference repo uses) is the modern choice and also fires for non-character keys.</li></ul>\n<h3>Challenge: show the pressed key in the h1</h3>\n<ul><li>Goal: when any key is pressed anywhere on the page, the h1 text becomes that key.</li><li>Solution: in the document-level key handler replace console.log with $(&quot;h1&quot;).text(event.key). It reuses the .text() method from the earlier lecture.</li></ul>\n<h3>The general .on() method</h3>\n<ul><li>$(selector).on(eventName, callback) takes the event type as a string and the handler as a second argument.</li><li>Any DOM event from the MDN event list works: &quot;mouseover&quot;, &quot;click&quot;, &quot;keydown&quot;, and so on.</li><li>Example: $(&quot;h1&quot;).on(&quot;mouseover&quot;, function () { $(&quot;h1&quot;).css(&quot;color&quot;, &quot;purple&quot;); }) changes colour when the pointer enters the heading.</li><li>It is more flexible than the shorthand methods since a single method covers every event type.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Vanilla JS vs jQuery for button clicks</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">// vanilla: loop required\nfor (var i = 0; i &lt; document.querySelectorAll(&quot;button&quot;).length; i++) {\n  document.querySelectorAll(&quot;button&quot;)[i].addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, function () {\n    document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).style.color = &quot;purple&quot;;\n  });\n}\n\n// jQuery: no loop\n$(&quot;button&quot;).click(function () {\n  $(&quot;h1&quot;).css(&quot;color&quot;, &quot;purple&quot;);\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Key presses and .on() (reference repo uses keydown and red/blue)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document.addEventListener(&quot;keydown&quot;, function (event) {\n  console.log(event.code);\n});\n\n// challenge solution: show the key in the h1\n$(document).keydown(function (event) {\n  $(&quot;h1&quot;).text(event.key);\n});\n\n$(&quot;h1&quot;).on(&quot;mouseover&quot;, function () {\n  $(&quot;h1&quot;).css(&quot;color&quot;, &quot;blue&quot;);\n});</pre>\n<h2>The task: Show the pressed key in the h1</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A page with an h1, five buttons, an input, jQuery loaded before the script, and a keypress listener that logs event.key.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Whenever a key is pressed anywhere on the page, display it in the h1.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Attach the key listener to the document, not just the input.</li><li>In the callback, select the h1.</li><li>Call .text() on it with event.key as the argument.</li><li>Reload, type, and see the heading change with every key.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The h1 always shows the last key pressed.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">$(document).keypress(function (event) {\n  $(&quot;h1&quot;).text(event.key);\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Do not wrap document in quotes: $(&quot;document&quot;) would look for an element named document.</li><li>With .keypress on a specific input, only typing inside that input is detected.</li><li>The reference repo's button colours differ (red/blue/purple) from the lecture's purple; it also mixes in a vanilla loop for comparison, and contains an author footer to ignore.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l157", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows how jQuery can insert new HTML into a live page with before(), after(), prepend() and append(), and delete elements with remove(). The four insertion methods differ only in where the new markup lands relative to the selected element.</p>\n<h3>Inserting elements: the four methods</h3>\n<ul><li>Select an element with $(), then call an insertion method and pass it a string of HTML. jQuery parses the string, builds the element(s) and places them in the DOM; the source .html file is never modified, so a page refresh removes them.</li><li>before(html) inserts the new markup as a sibling immediately in front of the selected element's opening tag (outside it).</li><li>after(html) inserts the markup as a sibling immediately behind the selected element's closing tag (outside it).</li><li>prepend(html) inserts the markup inside the selected element, as its first child, right after the opening tag and before the existing content.</li><li>append(html) inserts the markup inside the selected element, as its last child, after the existing content but before the closing tag.</li><li>Rule of thumb: before/after place content outside the element; prepend/append place it inside. Inspecting the page in dev tools makes the difference visible: a button added with prepend sits inside the h1 ahead of the text, one added with append sits inside the h1 behind the text.</li><li>The demo uses '&lt;button&gt;New&lt;/button&gt;' as the HTML string in each case, typed in the browser console against the live page.</li></ul>\n<h3>Removing elements</h3>\n<ul><li>remove() deletes every element in the jQuery selection from the DOM. $('button').remove() clears all button elements on the page in one go.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> remove() also discards the elements' attached event handlers and data; detach() is the variant that keeps them for re-insertion later.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Inserted HTML strings are real markup, so never build them from untrusted user input without escaping, or you open the door to script injection.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Insertion and removal methods on the h1 and buttons (matches the reference script)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">$(&quot;button&quot;).remove();                      // delete all buttons\n$(&quot;h1&quot;).before(&quot;&lt;button&gt;New&lt;/button&gt;&quot;);     // sibling above the h1\n$(&quot;h1&quot;).after(&quot;&lt;button&gt;New&lt;/button&gt;&quot;);      // sibling below the h1\n$(&quot;h1&quot;).prepend(&quot;&lt;button&gt;New&lt;/button&gt;&quot;);    // inside h1, before the text\n$(&quot;h1&quot;).append(&quot;&lt;button&gt;New&lt;/button&gt;&quot;);     // inside h1, after the text</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Resulting DOM for &lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt; after all four calls</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;button&gt;New&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;h1&gt;&lt;button&gt;New&lt;/button&gt;Hello&lt;button&gt;New&lt;/button&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;button&gt;New&lt;/button&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Confusing before/after (siblings) with prepend/append (children): a button prepended to an h1 becomes part of the heading.</li><li>Changes made this way live only in the running page; they vanish on reload because the HTML file is untouched.</li><li>In the reference repo the script lines are in the order remove, before, after, prepend, append; run remove() first and it will empty the page of the buttons that were there originally. The reference index.html also carries a personal footer credit that is not part of the lesson.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l158", "<p class=\"summary\">Tours jQuery's built-in effect methods (hide/show/toggle, fade, slide) and the custom animate() method, then shows how chaining queues effects on the same element. Closes by encouraging use of documentation rather than memorisation and leads into the Simon Game challenge.</p>\n<h3>Setup used in the demo</h3>\n<ul><li>The page has an h1 and several buttons; a click listener attached to all buttons runs an effect on the h1. The earlier css() colour change inside the handler is swapped for an effect method each time.</li><li>Pattern: $(&quot;button&quot;).on(&quot;click&quot;, function () { $(&quot;h1&quot;).&lt;effect&gt;(); });</li></ul>\n<h3>Hide, show, toggle</h3>\n<ul><li>hide() makes the selected element disappear instantly and removes it from layout flow, so following content moves up.</li><li>show() brings a hidden element back, equally abruptly.</li><li>toggle() flips between the two on each call, so any button click alternates the h1 between hidden and visible.</li></ul>\n<h3>Fade effects</h3>\n<ul><li>fadeOut() gradually lowers opacity, then hides the element; fadeIn() is the reverse, revealing the element while raising opacity to full.</li><li>fadeToggle() alternates between the two.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Each effect accepts an optional duration (milliseconds or 'slow'/'fast') and a callback that runs on completion; the default duration is 400 ms.</li></ul>\n<h3>Slide effects</h3>\n<ul><li>slideUp() collapses the element upward by animating its height down to zero; slideDown() expands it again; slideToggle() alternates.</li><li>Useful for things like drop-down menus or collapsible panels.</li></ul>\n<h3>Custom animation with animate()</h3>\n<ul><li>animate({ ... }) takes an object of CSS properties and gradually transitions the element to those target values, e.g. { opacity: 0.5 } fades it to half opacity.</li><li>Only numeric CSS properties can be animated. Trying to animate to a colour such as red fails (the keyword is treated as an undefined variable, giving a ReferenceError in the console, since it was written unquoted).</li><li>Numeric lengths work: { margin: 20 } in pixels, or a percentage supplied as a string such as &quot;20%&quot; (the reference uses animate({ margin: &quot;10%&quot; })).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Animating colours needs the jQuery UI or jQuery Color plugin; plain CSS transitions are the modern alternative.</li></ul>\n<h3>Chaining effects</h3>\n<ul><li>jQuery methods return the jQuery object, so calls can be linked on one selection: $(&quot;h1&quot;).slideUp().slideDown().animate({ opacity: 0.5 });</li><li>Effects on the same element are queued and run one after another, not simultaneously: here the h1 slides up, then slides down, then fades to 50% opacity.</li><li>Chaining also lets you select once and apply several actions.</li></ul>\n<h3>Don't memorise, look things up</h3>\n<ul><li>Guideline given: understand roughly what exists, then search the docs when needed. A web search such as 'jquery slide animation' brings up the official API docs with the full Effects list (animate, delay, fadeIn, fadeOut, fadeTo, slideDown, etc.).</li><li>jQuery is thoroughly documented on jquery.com, and Stack Overflow, W3Schools and MDN fill in the gaps.</li><li>The lecture ends by announcing the next section: a game built with jQuery as a boss-level challenge to consolidate the module.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">All effect methods, one active at a time (reference script keeps the others commented out)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">$(&quot;button&quot;).on(&quot;click&quot;, function () {\n  $(&quot;h1&quot;).hide();          // or show(), toggle()\n  // $(&quot;h1&quot;).fadeOut();    // fadeIn(), fadeToggle()\n  // $(&quot;h1&quot;).slideUp();    // slideDown(), slideToggle()\n  // $(&quot;h1&quot;).animate({ margin: &quot;10%&quot; });\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Chained effects run in sequence on the same element</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">$(&quot;button&quot;).on(&quot;click&quot;, function () {\n  $(&quot;h1&quot;).slideUp().slideDown().animate({ opacity: 0.5 });\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>animate() only handles numeric properties; colour values will not animate (and an unquoted word like red throws a ReferenceError).</li><li>Percentages and other units must be passed as strings, e.g. &quot;20%&quot;.</li><li>hide() removes the element from layout, shifting other content; fade effects end up hidden too, so layout still jumps at the end.</li><li>Reference index.html includes a personal footer credit that is not part of the course.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l159", "<p class=\"summary\">A short study tip: learning sticks better when topics from different modules are combined in small programs rather than practised only in isolation.</p>\n<h3>Interleave topics to strengthen recall</h3>\n<ul><li>Practising each topic only inside its own module builds weaker mental links than using several topics together.</li><li>Example pairing: conditionals (if/else) from one module with loops (for/while) from another.</li><li>Exercise to try: write a tiny program of roughly 5-10 lines that deliberately combines ideas from two different course modules, such as one from module 3 and one from module 5.</li><li>The program does not need to be impressive; the act of combining the ideas is what consolidates them.</li></ul>");
